a ceasefire appears to be holding  between Israel and the Palestinian  militants in Gaza after one of the worst  outbreaks of violence there in five  years  both sides have launched hundreds of  rockets and missiles at one another  since Friday more than 20 people have  been killed in Gaza and four more were  killed in Israel Seth Doane is in  Ashkelon Israel near the Gaza border  Seth good morning good morning there is  a sense of relief here this morning  after what residents tell us was an  absolutely terrifying 48 hours we are  inside one of the homes that was struck  by a Palestinian rocket which landed  just outside smashed these windows you  can see glass and debris on the floor  here and the man who owned the house was  killed Israel's military struck back  hitting about 350 targets in the Gaza  Strip one of those Palestinian targets  had been riding in this car Israel's  military says it was a senior Hamas  figure it was the first targeted  assassination by Israel since 2014  Palestinians say an infant was also  killed in one of the air strikes her  body was carried in a funeral procession  her father said the children were  playing and were struck by a missile  shot from a drone Palestinians fired  close to 700 rockets and projectiles  toward Israel about 200 of them were  intercepted but one hit near this house  well we see the substantial damage  Lieutenant Colonel Jonathan Cahn Rica's  showed us the damage today he says this  round of violence was sparked after  Israeli soldiers were shot at by  Palestinians during weekly Friday  protests at the border  these cease fires seem short-lived do  you have any confidence that this will  hold of trying to militarily destroy  Israel it leaves very little room for  optimism Palestinians condemned what  they called Israeli aggression amid an  unfolding humanitarian crisis in Gaza  this morning Israel's Prime Minister  Benjamin Netanyahu said that the  military campaign is not over yet Gail  thank you very much John reporting from  Israel
 

